# Logos Codex Instructions

This repository has Logos installed for Codex CLI.

## Default Workflow

This installed project uses Logos Nous Mode by default for coding tasks.
Apply the Logos Nous workflow to implementation, debugging, refactoring,
testing, review, and repository-maintenance requests.

The default orchestration path is Logos Runner plus Codex native subagents:

- `.\.logos\bin\logos-runner.cmd start --root . "<request>"`
- `.\.logos\bin\logos-runner.cmd next --root . <plan_id>`
- Use Codex `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent` for the prepared stage prompt.
- Save the subagent's final JSON, then run
  `.\.logos\bin\logos-runner.cmd record-stage --root . <plan_id> <stage> --file <json_file>`.
- Run `.\.logos\bin\logos-runner.cmd gate --root . <plan_id> <stage> --apply`.
- Repeat until the planning gate reaches execute, then run the execute and
  verify stages through the same native subagent/result/gate loop.

Do not manually recreate the Logos stage sequence when Runner can be used.
Runner owns stage prompts, plan state, gates, artifacts, and handoff files.
Codex native subagents own worker execution inside the current Codex session.
If the project-local Runner shim is missing or cannot run, stop before
implementation and report the installation blocker. Do not continue with a
Codex-only implementation plan.

## Skill Routing

Use `.agents/skills/nous/SKILL.md` as the primary Logos workflow for coding
tasks. This target intentionally installs only one auto-discoverable Codex
skill so Logos work is routed through the Nous director first.

Detailed step procedures live under `.agents/logos/procedures/` and are loaded
by Runner stage prompts or by `nous/SKILL.md` only for recovery/fallback.
Role cards live under `.agents/logos/roles/` and are also routed through
`nous/SKILL.md`; Codex should not treat them as standalone auto-selected skills.
Role detail references live under `.agents/logos/roles/references/`. Read a
detail reference only when the compact role card says to do so and the current
stage cannot be completed safely or clearly from the role card, procedure, and
official plan artifacts alone.
Soft rule cards live under `.agents/logos/rules/`. Runner selects relevant
rules through `.logos/generated/rules-manifest.json` and exposes compact
pointers in stage prompts. Do not read the whole rule library by default; read
only the specific rule card or `.agents/logos/rules/references/*-details.md`
file named by the active stage prompt when more detail is needed.

When `.logos/plans/<plan_id>/` artifacts exist, treat those artifacts as the
active work contract. Do not replace Runner-produced `task-plan.json`,
`context-handoff.json`, `execution-result.json`, or `verification-result.json`
with an ad hoc Codex plan.

Inside a plan directory, root JSON files are official contracts or final stage
outputs. Per-stage process records live under
`.logos/plans/<plan_id>/stages/<stage>/` as `prompt.md`, `raw.md`, and
`result.json`. Parse and validation failures live under
`.logos/plans/<plan_id>/errors/`.

## Operating Principles

- For coding tasks, prefer Runner-managed stage prompts over manual procedure execution.
- Follow the Runner stage gate: scan/exploration -> intake -> spec -> plan ->
  execute -> verify.
- Prefer Runner-produced artifacts for stage state and handoff.
- Do not call nested `codex exec` from inside Codex. Use native subagent tools
  when worker separation is needed.
- Persisted Logos artifacts under `.logos/` must be written in English,
  regardless of the user's input language.
- Translate or summarize user-provided requirements into English for official
  JSON artifacts.
- Use `.logos/plans/<plan_id>/user-answers.jsonl` as the only official user
  clarification log; do not create ad hoc clarification files.
- Subagents return JSON to the parent Codex session. They must not directly
  create or edit official stage result files under `.logos/plans/<plan_id>/`.
- Official result JSON is created only by `logos-runner record-stage`.
- After spawning a native subagent, avoid repeated empty wait updates. If no
  agent has completed, report that at most once per stage, then wait quietly or
  provide only meaningful status changes.
- Do not relay raw repeated wait noise such as `Waiting for agents`,
  `Finished waiting`, or `No agents completed yet` more than once for the same
  stage. Prefer one concise stage-status sentence, then report only completion,
  blocker, approval request, or material state change.
- Do not edit files, run build/test commands, or start implementation before
  Runner planning has produced `next_step: executor`.
- Read `.agents/logos/procedures/execution.md` only after the planning gate
  returns `next_step: executor`, or when Runner explicitly builds an execution
  prompt from it.
- Inspect before edits.
- Keep changes scoped to the user request.
- Plan before implementation.
- Review risk before sensitive or irreversible actions.
- Verify with concrete evidence before final response.
- Report skipped or unavailable checks plainly.

## Enforcement Boundary

Use `.agents/logos/procedures/review.md` as the source of truth for guard
enforcement status. Keep this file as routing guidance and put detailed
procedures in `.agents/logos/procedures/` and role boundaries in
`.agents/logos/roles/`. Treat `.agents/logos/rules/` as soft guidance only;
hard runtime enforcement belongs to hooks, guards, approval policy, and Runner
gates.

## Work-State Recovery

Logos records low-cost work state under `.logos/`. Do not read
`.logos/runs/` or `.logos/evidence/` by default.

When continuing after context loss, compaction, or an unclear work state, read
`.logos/memory/resume-snapshot.md` first. If that is insufficient, read
`.logos/memory/active-work.json`, `.logos/memory/run-index.json`, and only then
the specific plan or run record needed. Raw evidence logs are for verification
or debugging, not routine context loading.

